[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was filed as one parser bug. It was built by merging on subject
lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so it holds every report that
sounded alike. Read by tenant, booking form and body, it holds more than one problem. Each one
below has a ticket of its own.

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser fix shipped ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
locales"), and the hint won't be fixed because the booking-v3 form is retired. Neither change
touches the one-day move, so its ticket stays open.

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="ra88github/what-it-shows" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day': 3 }} outcome={{ parser: 'fixed', hint: "won't fix: booking-v3 retired" }} />

## How they were told apart

**parser.** The tenant writes the day first, and the report came in through the app, by email,
or through a booking-v2 form, whose hint asks for DD/MM/YYYY. The shared parser reads every date
month first, so day and month swap: a 7 April reminder goes out on 4 July. This is the bug #4471
describes. Fixed: the parser now reads day-first dates for EU locales.

**hint.** The tenant runs booking-v3. Its form prints MM/DD/YYYY under the date field, people
type the month first as told, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The
symptom is the same swap, but the cause is the form, not the parser, so the parser fix does not
reach these reports. Won't fix: booking-v3 is retired. Only reports that came through the form
belong here: the same tenants' app and email reports are `parser`.

**one-day.** These dates move by one day, not by a swap. Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May.
A client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and a client in Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting. An
invite arrives before the booking was made. A month/day swap cannot do any of that; it points to
a date passed through a time zone. The desk had already listed these reports as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`). Like the cause table on #4471, this page still counts them under `parser`,
where their tenants file them, and gives them a row and a ticket of their own as well. The
parser fix does not change a time zone, so these stay open after #4471 closed.

## Why the queue hid them

- **Subject lines.** Merging on subject joins reports that sound alike, whatever their cause.
  Different bugs make people write the same words: "wrong day", "date is wrong".
- **Intake tags.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the
  tenant: where they are and which booking form they run. The *intake tagged as* column shows how
  far the two drift.
- **Repeated rows.** The ticket system exports in pages that overlap, so a report can appear
  twice. Every count on this page is by report id.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not everything in it is fixed. The one-day move is still open, on its own ticket.
</Callout>

## For the desk

- Triage by cause, not by subject. Tenant, channel and booking form decide the cause, and the
  body can overrule them: a one-day move is never a swap. That needs a new decision record to
  replace 0001, which stays as it was accepted.
- Closing a merged ticket closes only what its fixes reach. Check every row of this table
  against what shipped before closing the rest: here the one-day move is still open.

